# Per-tenant topic ACL — FGMP development/test broker.
#
# Read this alongside mosquitto.conf: the config file decides that these rules
# are ENFORCED; this file is what they say.
#
# ── THE RULE SHAPE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A grant is `topic <tenant-subtree>`. The `#` terminates the pattern, so
#
#     topic fgmp/tenant-a/#
#
# covers exactly `fgmp/tenant-a/**` and nothing else. MQTT topic filters match
# level by level, so there is no way for that pattern to spill into
# `fgmp/tenant-b/...` — a subscription to tenant B's subtree matches zero of
# tenant A's grants, and is refused.
#
# The absence of a leading `%u` or `+` wildcard matters. A rule written as
# `topic fgmp/%u/#` looks tidier and is WRONG here: it would grant every user
# access to every tenant's subtree, because the function is intended to be
# bound to the user's own namespace.
#
# ── CHANNELS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each tenant may:
#   - read   its telemetry and status        (subscriber: dashboards, gateway)
#   - write  its telemetry and status        (publisher: field devices)
#   - read   its own `command` topic        (device executes downlink)
#   - write  its own `command/ack`          (device acknowledges downlink)
#
# A tenant canNOT publish to another tenant's telemetry, and canNOT read
# another tenant's commands. Those would be the two directions that turn
# telemetry spoofing into a real attack: forge a fuel level, or issue a
# generator start command to someone else's set.

# ── tenant-a ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `readwrite` is REQUIRED, not decorative: a device publishes telemetry and
# subscribes for its own downlinks. These lines were previously written
#
#     topic readwrite fgmp/tenant-a/
#
# — a trailing slash and no `#`. That is a malformed topic filter, not a
# shorthand: a trailing `/` matches only the literal empty level that follows
# it, so the grant covered `fgmp/tenant-a/` itself and nothing beneath it. The
# effect was the opposite of what this file claimed: tenant-a could READ its
# subtree but not PUBLISH into it, and every upload failed with MQTT reason
# code 135 (Not authorized).
#
# It stayed invisible to the isolation tests, which only assert that one tenant
# cannot see another — a permission that was never granted in the first place,
# so it never failed. Nothing in the suite published as a tenant into its own
# subtree until the same-tenant delivery assertion was added.
user tenant-a
topic readwrite fgmp/tenant-a/#
topic read fgmp/tenant-a/#

# ── tenant-b ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
user tenant-b
topic readwrite fgmp/tenant-b/#
topic read fgmp/tenant-b/#

# ── platform (internal ops) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ops is cross-tenant BY DESIGN and is explicitly NOT the application role.
# It exists for support and diagnostics, mirroring the fgmp_ops database role.
# It is kept on a separate connection and must never be present in the web
# app's environment — the same rule that governs FGMP_OPS_DATABASE_URL.
user platform
topic readwrite fgmp/platform/#
topic read fgmp/#

# ── NO CATCH-ALL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is deliberately no `topic read #` for anyone. An ACL file's implicit
# behaviour for an unmatched request is to DENY, and that default is the
# control. Adding a permissive trailing rule would turn every rule above into
# decoration.